TEST MATERIAL ONLY — NOT A CREDENTIAL. THIS KEY SECURES NOTHING.

  This is the private half of test/fixtures/loopback-cert.pem: a self-signed
  EC prime256v1 key for CN=127.0.0.1, minted once out of band so that
  test/harness/interceptor.ts can run an HTTPS origin on the loopback address
  and observe the production outbound client (FR-75).

  It is deliberately committed in cleartext. It has never been presented to a
  real service, it certifies only 127.0.0.1, and the only client that ever
  speaks to it is a test process that has already disabled certificate
  verification via UNIFI_LOCAL_TLS_INSECURE=true — so the key material is not
  what makes the handshake succeed, and replacing it with any other key would
  work equally well.

  It exists because Node's standard library cannot mint an X.509 certificate,
  a dev dependency is forbidden by FR-75, and `openssl` at test time is not
  portable to the windows-latest CI leg. The full rationale and the exact
  regeneration command are in the banner of loopback-cert.pem.

  TO A SECRET SCANNER: this finding is a true positive about the file type and
  a false positive about the risk. Allow-list this exact path. Do not train
  reviewers to dismiss private-key findings generally.

